# Prototyping Guide - Healing v4.6 Land Version

**Gali-Spinal Tube Resonant Vortex Reactor**  
**Antenna Base + Galinstan Microchannels + EM Pump + Temp Control**

## Safety First (Non-Negotiable)
- Work in a well-ventilated area
- Wear gloves and eye protection when handling Galinstan
- Galinstan can cause liquid metal embrittlement on aluminum and some other metals — use compatible materials only (TiC, PTFE, Nitinol, 316L stainless, glass)
- Keep Galinstan contained — it has a tendency to "creep"
- Use proper electrical safety (fuses, BMS, insulation) on watt stacking output
- Never claim or imply over-unity / free energy — this is a supplemental trickle system only

## Recommended Materials (v4.6 Healing Version)
- TiC + PTFE composite tube (~65mm diameter, ~380mm length)
- Nitinol or 316L car-antenna style whip base with integrated Galinstan microchannels
- Electromagnetic (MHD) pump components (electrodes + magnets)
- Highly purified magnetite spheres (2–8mm)
- 40% DMSO + Galinstan + 8–12% natural tree resin/sap carrier fluid
- Double-headed sliced ferrite starburst core
- Peltier modules + insulation for 25–35°C temperature jacket
- Piezo elements + tree resin/sap for self-healing foundation
- Magnetite for grounding layer

## Prototyping Path (Recommended Order)

### Phase 1: Desktop Proof-of-Concept (Small Scale)
1. Build a small magnetic vortex orb version first (easier to test)
2. Test whip motion + sphere whipping behavior
3. Verify MR resin carrier viscosity and self-healing at room temp
4. Test basic piezo response from foundation
5. Document RPM range and visual "spheres’ song" effects

### Phase 2: Full Gali-Spinal Tube (Bench Top)
1. Assemble TiC/PTFE tube + ferrite core
2. Install Nitinol antenna base with Galinstan microchannels
3. Integrate compact EM pump
4. Add temperature control jacket (Peltier + sensors)
5. Fill with optimized carrier fluid (10% resin mix)
6. Test at controlled 25–35°C
7. Measure output, resonance, and self-healing behavior

### Phase 3: Land / Vehicle Integration
1. Mount on stable base with piezo-sap foundation
2. Add proper electrical isolation and fusing
3. Connect to watt-stacked battery bank via charge controller
4. Test in real vibration environment (road or land)
5. Monitor temperature stability and long-term behavior

## Tools & Equipment
- Basic hand tools + precision measuring
- Multimeter + oscilloscope (for resonance and piezo output)
- Temperature controller / PID
- Safe container for Galinstan handling
- 3D printer or machining capability (helpful for custom parts)
- Notebook + camera for documentation

## Testing & Validation Goals
- Stable operation in 25–35°C range
- Clear "spheres’ song" harmonics
- Measurable gentle pulsed output (120–220W target)
- Visible self-healing resin migration
- Good Earth grounding through foundation
- Safe, contained operation with no leaks

## Next Milestones
- Desktop orb working reliably
- Full tube with antenna base running stable
- Real-world land/vehicle test with watt stacking
- Documented Time Echo / resonance visuals
- Refined material mix based on real testing

## Notes
This is an iterative, hands-on creation process. Start small, document everything, and refine based on real results. The 25–35°C temperature window is critical for performance.

Stay curious, stay safe, and keep building.

